.TH UFLIB 7 "November 2026" "uflib 4.1.0" "UFSRV Programmer's Manual"
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH NAME
uflib \- foundational C library for the ufsrv messaging-server platform
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <uflib/utils.h>
.B #include <uflib/utils_crypto.h>
.B #include <uflib/adt/adt_hashtable.h>
.B #include <uflib/cdt/cdt_mpsc_queue.h>
.B #include <uflib/cdt/lockless_ringbuffer/cdt_lockless_ringbuffer.h>
.B #include <uflib/cdt/alias_map/alias_map.h>
.B #include <uflib/recycler_v2/recycler_v2.h>
.B #include <uflib/logger/logger.h>
.B #include <uflib/ufconfig/ufconfig.h>
.B #include <uflib/ufcommand/ufcommand.h>
.B #include <uflib/buffer_descriptor/buffer_descriptor.h>
.B #include <uflib/resource_router/resource_router.h>
.B #include <uflib/optional/ufoptional.h>
.B #include <uflib/msg_framing/websocket/ws_frame.h>
.B #include <uflib/msg_framing/websocket/ws_message.h>
.B #include <uflib/msg_framing/websocket/ws_close.h>
.B #include <uflib/msg_framing/websocket/ws_handshake.h>
.fi
.sp
Link with
.BR \-luflib .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uflib
is the bottom layer of the ufsrv development stack.  It provides the
general-purpose software primitives every higher layer builds on: abstract
data types, lock-free concurrent data structures, an object recycler,
crypto and DB abstractions, networking helpers, an owning optional
container, a path-keyed route registry, a command registry, a
schema-driven configuration loader,
compression, JSON, protobuf, UUID generation, a backend-agnostic logging
interface, an RFC 6455 WebSocket codec, and utility routines.  Dependencies flow
strictly downward \(em uflib has no visibility of ufsrvcorelib, ufnetcorelib,
or any application server.
.PP
uflib builds
.B libuflib_static.a
(and, when
.BR BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON ,
.BR libuflib.so )
from a single OBJECT library.  It is consumed by the higher layers via
.BR "find_package(uflib REQUIRED)" " + " uflib::uflib .
The library is written in C17 with GNU extensions, requires Clang, and is
licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License, version 3 or later.
.PP
This page describes version
.BR 4.1.0 " of "
.BR uflib ,
that being
.BR MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
as defined in the installed
.BR version.h .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH MODULES
.\" ===================================================
.SS Abstract data types \&(adt) \(em \fBadt/\fP
Classical single-threaded containers.
.PP
.TP
.B hashtable_v2
Open-addressing hash table (linear probing), growable or fixed, optionally
locked, optionally unique-keyed.
.BR HashTableInstantiate ()
and
.BR HashTableLockingInstantiate ()
fix the key configuration, which cannot change afterwards:
.I offset
is
.IR offsetof (Item, key_field)
within the extracted item;
.I key_size
is the field width for a fixed-width key
.RB ( sizeof(unsigned long))
or
.B KEY_SIZE_ZERO
for a NUL-terminated string;
.I key_is_ptr
is
.B HASHTABLE_KEY_IS_PTR
when that field holds a pointer to the key
.RB ( "char *name" )
and
.B HASHTABLE_KEY_IS_INLINE
when the key is stored inline
.RB ( "char name[N]" ", " "unsigned long id" ).
Extraction is a separate dimension: pass
.IR item_extractor_callback
when callers hold containers
.RB ( "InstanceHolder *" )
rather than items, and the table maps container to item on every operation.
.PP
Insertion returns the container that became resident; with unique keys it also
reports whether this call claimed the key and, when not, who holds it
.RB ( AddToHashUniquely ).
.PP
Pitfalls: the legacy names
.B HASH_ITEM_IS_PTR_TYPE
and
.B HASH_ITEM_NOT_PTR_TYPE
describe the key, not the container. Give the API exactly one level of
indirection \(em containers to a table with an extractor, extracted items to one
without; the wrong level makes every key read address the wrong object.  A
unique-key table compares key values, so a resident item's key must not be
mutated.
.TP
.B hopscotch_hashtable_v2
Hopscotch hash table (bounded probe sequences).
.TP
.B distinct_array
Array of unique elements.
.TP
.B linkedlist / doubly_linkedlist
Intrusive singly- and doubly-linked lists.
.TP
.B queue
FIFO queue.
.TP
.B minheap
Single-threaded binary min-heap (priority queue).  Opaque handle; pointer-key
and inline-int64 key modes; any-magnitude comparator; geometric growth.
.BR MinHeapDescribe (MinHeap, bd)
emits state as JSON (size, capacity, mode, load factor, entries).
.TP
.B locking_lru
Mutex-protected least-recently-used cache.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Concurrent data types \&(cdt) \(em \fBcdt/\fP
Lock-free and lock-based structures for multi-threaded hot paths, each
declared in its own header under
.IR cdt/ .
.PP
.TP
.B LamportQueue
Lock-free SPSC FIFO (two-index, one reserved slot).
.TP
.B LocklessMpscQueue
Intrusive lock-free MPSC (Vyukov).
.TP
.B LocklessFixedWidthHashMap
Lock-free fixed-width hash map (seq-lock + open addressing).
.TP
.B LocklessRingBuffer
Bounded copy-in/copy-out ring buffer with three concurrency modes \(em
SPSC, MPSC, and MPMC (Vyukov per-slot sequence numbers).
.TP
.B LocklessLru
Lock-free approximate least-recently-used cache (CLOCK second-chance
eviction, per-slot seqlocks, upsert
.BR LocklessLruSet / LocklessLruSetEx ).
.TP
.B LocklessMinHeap
Lock-free integer min-priority queue.  The concurrent companion to the
single-threaded
.BR MinHeap ,
and deliberately not a binary heap: a sift is a multi-word update on a
path and cannot be linearised portably, so this is a Harris lock-free
ordered list.  Inline
.B int64_t
keys with an opaque value, insert and delete-min only, no peek; reclamation
is quiescent
.RB ( LocklessMinHeapReclaim ).
.TP
.B ChaseLevStealingQueue
Chase-Lev work-stealing deque.
.TP
.B spinlock_anderson_t / SpinlockMcs
Anderson and MCS locks, with the spin-pause definitions they share in
.IR cdt_spinlock_defs.h .
.TP
.B LocklessTreiberStack
Shared ABA-protected intrusive LIFO free-list (used by the recycler and
hash map).
.TP
.B AliasMap
Thread-safe many-to-one map from alias strings to one shared value.  Alias
groups are the unit of mutation: aliases can be added, moved between groups
and removed without disturbing their siblings, and a group exists exactly
while it holds at least one alias.  Lookups either allocate a copy
.RB ( AliasMapGetCopy )
or write into a caller buffer
.RB ( AliasMapGetInto ,
which distinguishes absent, too-small and invalid through
.BR errno );
.B AliasMapAliasesOf
returns a group snapshot in one allocation,
.B AliasMapSnapshotCreate
copies every group and alias into one immutable snapshot the caller walks with
.BR AliasMapSnapshotGroupCount (),
.BR AliasMapSnapshotValueAt (),
.BR AliasMapSnapshotAliasCountAt ()
and
.BR AliasMapSnapshotAliasAt ()
before
.BR AliasMapSnapshotDestroy (),
.B AliasMapForEach
streams the same groups through a caller visitor without allocating,
and
.B AliasMapDescribe
emits JSON metadata.
.PP
Maps are created empty
.RB ( AliasMapCreate ,
.BR AliasMapCreateWithLogger ),
from a collector descriptor
.RB ( AliasMapCreateWithDescriptor )
or from a JSON document
.RB ( AliasMapCreateWithJsonDescriptor ).
A logger is bound once at construction and never changed; bulk construction
takes no lock because the map is private until the call returns, rejects
duplicate aliases, pre-sizes the bucket table and either publishes a complete
map or returns NULL with the reason and coordinates in
.BR AliasMapCreateResult .
The JSON form is an array of
.B {"value": string, "keys": [string, ...]}
objects, parsed strictly by uflib itself with no external JSON dependency and
with byte offsets reported for malformed input.
.PP
Every alias and value is copied, so a map never retains caller memory.  The
construction origin (incremental, descriptor or JSON) is fixed at creation and
reported by
.BR AliasMapGetOrigin ().
.BR AliasMapDescribe ( AliasMap ", " BufferDescriptor )
emits origin, bulk flag, group and alias counts, key and value byte totals,
bucket geometry, load factor, group-size statistics, maximum chain length,
logger state and a bounded group listing, appending to a caller-supplied
descriptor when one is passed.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Recycler \&(recycler, recycler_v2)
Object pooling with optional persistence.  A recycler allocates objects in
slab-like groups, hands them out through a lock-free free stack, and \(em in
V2 \(em can CLOCK-evict idle objects to disk and re-materialise them on
demand.
.PP
.TP
.B RecyclerV2InitTypePool(config)
Register a type pool.
.TP
.B RecyclerV2Get(handle, ctx, flags)
Lease an object (returns an
.BR InstanceHolderV2 ).
.TP
.B RecyclerV2Put(ih, ctx, flags)
Return an object to the pool.
.TP
.B RecyclerV2Referenced(ih, n) / RecyclerV2UnReferenced(ih, n)
External reference counting (multi-reference).
.TP
.B RecyclerV2GetNewInstance(ih) / RecyclerV2DestroyInstance(ih)
Multi-holder aliases for sharing one object.
.TP
.B RecyclerV2GetCapacity(handle) / RecyclerV2GetLeasedCount(handle)
Capacity and occupancy queries.
.TP
.B DescribeRecycler(type_pool_name, provided)
Describe pool state as a JSON string (all pools, or one by exact name).
.TP
.B ListRecyclerTypes()
List every registered type name in one contiguous allocation (free once).
.TP
.B RecyclerV2DumpState(stream) / RecyclerV2DumpStateToBuffer()
Full debug/introspection snapshot as JSON.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Buffer descriptor \&(buffer_descriptor)
A growable string builder used for console/JSON output.
.PP
.TP
.B BufferDescriptorInit(bd, cap)
Allocate the backing buffer (clamped to [64, 1 MiB]).
.TP
.B BufferDescriptorAppendFormatted(bd, fmt, ...)
Append printf-style output, growing as needed.
.TP
.B BufferDescriptorRelease(bd)
Free the backing buffer.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Optional \&(optional)
An opaque container holding either one payload or nothing, following the
semantics of Java's
.B Optional
where they survive the crossing into C.  The payload is a
.BR "void *" ,
and every operation that can fail reports a
.B UfOptionalStatus
rather than throwing.
.sp
The container
.B owns
its payload.  A constructor takes ownership of a non-NULL payload on success,
.BR UfOptionalMap " and " UfOptionalFlatMap
take ownership of what the mapper publishes, and
.B UfOptionalTake
hands ownership back to the caller.  Release and transfer both go through the
destroy callback fixed when the payload was stored, so the container never has
to know what kind of payload it holds.  A NULL handle is
.B not
a container: every read accessor tolerates one and reports empty, so a host
that has not yet built an optional needs no guard branch at each use.
.sp
.TP
.B UfOptionalEmpty(destroy, context) / UfOptionalOf(value, destroy, context) / UfOptionalOfNullable(value, destroy, context)
Constructors.  The first two yield an empty container and reject a NULL
payload; the third collapses a NULL payload to empty.
.TP
.B UfOptionalClone(source, clone, destroy, context)
Deep-copy through a caller-supplied clone operation.
.TP
.B UfOptionalDestroy(optional)
Destroy a container, releasing the payload it still owns.
.TP
.B UfOptionalIsPresent(optional) / UfOptionalIsEmpty(optional) / UfOptionalGet(optional)
State and borrowed access.
.TP
.B UfOptionalTake(optional)
Take the payload, leaving the container empty.
.TP
.B UfOptionalOrElse(optional, other) / UfOptionalOrElseGet(optional, supplier, context)
The payload, or a fallback.  The supplier is consulted only when it is needed.
.TP
.B UfOptionalOrElseThrow(optional, out_value)
Java's
.BR orElseThrow ,
expressed as a status and an out-parameter.
.TP
.B UfOptionalIfPresent(optional, consumer, context) / UfOptionalFilter(optional, predicate, context)
Act on the payload; discard it unless it satisfies a predicate.
.TP
.B UfOptionalMap(optional, mapper, context, mapped_destroy, out_optional) / UfOptionalFlatMap(optional, mapper, context, out_optional)
Transform the payload into a new container, flat in the second case.  A mapper
that publishes a payload and
.B then
reports failure leaves the release to the library, so it must not release the
value itself.  The context given to the mapper is the one later handed to the
mapped payload's destroy callback.
.TP
.B UfOptionalSet(optional, value, destroy, context)
Replace the payload.  Re-setting the payload the container already holds is a
no-op on the payload itself.
.TP
.B UfOptionalLastStatus(optional)
The outcome of the most recent state-changing call.  Reads never record, and
neither do calls that found nothing to do:
.BR UfOptionalTake " and " UfOptionalFilter
record
.B UFOPTIONAL_OK
when they had a payload to work on and
.B UFOPTIONAL_EMPTY
when the container was already empty.
.sp
What the module does not do: it is not thread-safe.  A container is
single-owner, and if one is shared between threads synchronization belongs at
that boundary, outside this API.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Resource router \&(resource_router)
A path-keyed registry: a tree of route paths, each carrying per-method
callbacks.  A consumer declares its routes in a table instead of hand-writing a
parser, then hands one path through the registry to get the handler and the
unparsed tail back.
.sp
The registry is
.B an ADT only
\(em no threads, no locks, no logging, no global state.  Every registry is a
separate heap handle, so one process may hold several.  Registration completes
before matching starts; concurrent matching on a frozen registry is safe,
registration racing a match is a caller error.
.sp
Paths are
.B '/'-separated segments
in a sorted trie, so sibling lookup is a binary search and a route may be the
prefix of another.  A path registered with
.B RESOURCE_ROUTE_STEMMED
short-circuits the walk: the handler receives everything after the stem's last
segment and does its own parsing.  A registered node shadowing the nodes below
it is intentional.
.sp
A match answers in three states, so an unknown path and an unsupported method
are never confused:
.BR RESOURCE_ROUTE_MATCH ,
.B RESOURCE_ROUTE_METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED ,
.BR RESOURCE_ROUTE_NO_MATCH .
.sp
.TP
.B ResourceRouteRegistryCreate() / ResourceRouteRegistryDestroy(registry)
Allocate and release a registry.  Destroying NULL is a no-op.
.TP
.B ResourceRouteRegister(registry, path, description, handler, flags)
Register one route.  The handler descriptor and description are copied, so both
may live on the caller's stack.  Malformed paths, an empty method mask, unknown
bits and duplicate paths are refused without touching the registry.
.TP
.B ResourceRouteRegisterTable(registry, table, n)
Register every row of a static
.B ResourceRouteEntry
table.  Every row is validated before any is registered, so a bad row leaves
the registry untouched; the return value is the 1-based index of the failing
row.
.TP
.B ResourceRouteMatch(registry, method, path, handler, remainder)
Resolve one path under one method.  On
.B RESOURCE_ROUTE_MATCH
the callback is returned through
.I handler
and, for a stemmed route, the unparsed tail through
.IR remainder .
The tail points into the caller's path and is valid only for the call; it is
NULL for an exact, non-stemmed match.  No allocation takes place.
.TP
.B ResourceRouteRegistryDescribe(registry, buffer)
Write a JSON description of the registry into an initialised
.BR BufferDescriptor :
one object per level keyed by segment, with
.BR name ,
.BR description ,
.B methods
and
.B stemmed
per route, and a
.B children
object where a route has descendants.  Returns NULL rather than truncated JSON.
.sp
.B Example
.nf
ResourceRouteRegistry *routes = ResourceRouteRegistryCreate();
ResourceRouteHandler   handler = { .on_get = onGet, .methods = RESOURCE_ROUTE_METHOD_GET };
static const ResourceRouteEntry table[] = {
    { "suspend",        "suspend work",    &handler, 0 },
    { "suspend/thread1", "one thread",     &handler, 0 },
};

ResourceRouteRegisterTable(routes, table, 2);

const ResourceRouteHandler *hit;
const char                 *remainder;
switch (ResourceRouteMatch(routes, RESOURCE_ROUTE_METHOD_GET, "suspend/thread1", &hit, &remainder)) {
case RESOURCE_ROUTE_MATCH:              /* hit->on_get(ctx) */            break;
case RESOURCE_ROUTE_METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED: /* 405 */                          break;
case RESOURCE_ROUTE_NO_MATCH:           /* 404 */                          break;
}
.fi
.sp
.SS Utilities \&(utils*.h)
String, file, crypto, time, thread, and encoding helpers:
.BR utils_str ,
.BR utils_file ,
.BR utils_crypto ,
.BR utils_secrets ,
.BR utils_time ,
.BR utils_threads ,
.BR utils_base64 ,
.BR utils_base64url ,
.BR base32 ,
.BR utils_hex ,
.BR utils_iec_quantity ,
.BR utils_urls ,
.BR utils_bits ,
.BR utils_collection_str ,
.BR utils_sys ,
and
.BR tokeniser .
.PP
.B utils_secrets
carries the AES-256-GCM envelope primitives used by
.BR ufconfig 's
encrypted fields: it produces and consumes
.B enc:v1:<nonce>:<ciphertext>:<tag>
values, reads a secrets file mapping a field's path to its key, and wipes key
material and plaintext.  It is usable on its own by anything that needs an
envelope rather than only by the configuration module.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Persistence and networking \&(db, net)
.B net/
provides networking helpers and resolves OpenSSL through
.BR find_package(OpenSSL) .
.PP
.B db/
is a backend-agnostic SQL abstraction.  Its dispatcher,
.IR db_abstraction.c ,
is compiled when either driver is, and each driver is opt-in:
.TP
.B UFLIB_CAPABILITY_SQL
The MariaDB/MySQL driver,
.IR db_mysql.c .
.TP
.B UFLIB_CAPABILITY_SQLITE
The SQLite driver,
.IR db_sqlite.c ,
which defines
.B _HOEL_SQLITE
for every consumer of
.BR db_sql.h .
.PP
A backend is chosen by the constructor called, not by a type argument.
.TP
.B h_connect_mariadb(host, user, passwd, db, port, socket) / h_connect_sqlite(path)
Return a
.B "struct _h_connection *"
whose
.B type
is
.B HOEL_DB_TYPE_MARIADB
or
.BR HOEL_DB_TYPE_SQLITE .
Every later call dispatches on that field, so the rest of an application is
backend-blind.
.TP
.B h_execute_query(conn, sql, result, options)
The single entry point for both directions:
.B H_OPTION_SELECT
fills
.IR result ,
.B H_OPTION_EXEC
does not.
.TP
.B h_query_select / h_query_insert / h_query_update / h_query_delete
Conveniences over that call, each rejecting a string that does not contain its
own verb.  DDL therefore goes through
.BR h_execute_query " with " H_OPTION_EXEC .
.TP
.B h_query_last_insert_id(conn) / h_escape_string(conn, value)
The last row id, and a caller-freed escaped copy of a value.
.TP
.B h_clean_result(&result) / h_close_db(conn) / h_clean_connection(conn)
Release a result, close a handle, release the handle itself.  The three are
distinct: closing does not free the descriptor.
.PP
A result is a row-major array of type-tagged cells:
.B _h_result
holds
.B nb_rows
and
.BR nb_columns ,
and each cell's
.B _h_data.type
is one of
.BR HOEL_COL_TYPE_INT ,
.BR DOUBLE ,
.BR TEXT ,
.BR BLOB ,
.B DATE
or
.BR NULL ,
with the payload behind
.BR t_data .
Integer payloads are signed 64-bit, matching the storage they come from.
.PP
A string may hold several statements, and every verb runs all of them.  A
batch is atomic by default: it is wrapped in a transaction and rolled back if
any statement fails.
.B h_execute_query_ex
and
.B h_execute_query_collect_ex
take an
.B is_atomic
argument, false restoring partial application.  A batch that issues its own
.B BEGIN
or
.B COMMIT
needs that false, since a transaction cannot be nested.
.PP
Where several statements return rows,
.B h_execute_query_collect
returns one
.B _h_result
per statement in a
.BR CollectionDescriptor ,
indexed by statement ordinal, and reports the failing ordinal in
.IR failed_index .
Statements producing no columns still occupy an entry, with
.B nb_rows
and
.B nb_columns
zero.
.B h_clean_results
owns the teardown.
.PP
Values are not to be pasted into SQL.
.B UfDbStatementPrepare
prepares a single statement and refuses a string holding a second, so a value
can never extend the statement it belongs to; the bind verbs take it out of
the SQL text altogether.
.TP
.B UfDbStatementBindInt / BindDouble / BindText / BindBlob / BindNull
Bind by parameter index, counting from one.
.TP
.B UfDbStatementStep(statement, &has_row) / UfDbStatementColumn(statement, index)
Step one row at a time, and read a cell of the current row as
.BR _h_data ,
freed with
.BR h_clean_data_full .
.TP
.B UfDbStatementReset / UfDbStatementClearBindings / UfDbStatementFinalize
Reuse a prepared statement, drop its bindings, or release it.
.PP
The statement surface is SQLite only.  MariaDB statements remain reachable
through
.BR h_statement_prepare ,
which returns the driver's own statement type.
.PP
The JSON queries the abstraction once carried are not built: their
declarations are disabled and no driver implements them.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Ufsrv identifier \&(ufsrvuid)
The ufsrv-native user identifier: a timestamp, instance and sequence tuple
with reserved system-user values.  Generated, converted to and from binary and
encoded text, and compared.
.PP
.TP
.B UfsrvUidGenerate(uid) / UfsrvUidCopy(dst, src)
Create an identifier, or copy one.
.TP
.B UfsrvUidConvertToBinary(uid, buf) / UfsrvUidConvertSerialise(uid, buf)
Binary and serialised forms.
.TP
.B UfsrvUidCreateFromBinary(buf) / UfsrvUidCreateFromEncodedText(text)
Reconstruct an identifier from either form.
.TP
.B UfsrvUidGetTimestamp(uid) / UfsrvUidGetInstanceId(uid) / UfsrvUidGetSequenceId(uid)
The components the identifier is built from.
.TP
.B UfsrvUidIsSystemUser(uid) / UfsrvUidRawDataSystemUser()
The reserved system-user range.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Scheduled jobs \&(scheduled_jobs)
Scheduled task execution.
.\" ===================================================
.SS File loader \&(file_loader, thread_worker)
Caching a file's contents and reloading it when it changes, plus a concurrent
service that keeps a bounded registry of files mapped and watches each one for
modification.
.PP
.TP
.B FileLoaderLoadIfModified(loader, path) / FileLoaderReset(loader)
Load on change; drop the cached copy (synchronous, per-caller loader).
.TP
.B FileLoaderCompareHashes(a, b)
Detect change by content digest rather than by timestamp.
.PP
The concurrent service is configured through
.B FileLoaderServiceConcurrent
(registry size, hot-loaded size, blacklist, process name) and identified by an
opaque
.B FileLoaderService
handle:
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceInit / FileLoaderServiceInitWithLogger / FileLoaderServiceDestroy
Start the service and its monitor thread, or stop it.
.B FileLoaderServiceInitWithLogger
binds a caller-owned
.B UfLogger
for diagnostics.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceAddFile / FileLoaderServiceRemoveFile
Register a path (idempotent \(em a second add returns the existing handle) or
drop it.  A blacklisted path is refused with handle 0.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceGetFileInfo
Snapshot of one path's state: registered, loaded, recently accessed, size and
state (active, deleted, blacklisted, too_large, load_error, evicted).
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceGetFileContent / FileLoaderServiceGetFileContentByPath
Fetch a published buffer by handle or by registered path.  The buffer remains
valid until returned; the path form refuses blacklisted files with
.B EACCES
and unknown paths with
.BR ENOENT .
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceReturnFile
Release a buffer obtained from either content getter.  At most one buffer may
be pinned per thread at a time.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceSize / FileLoaderServiceCapacity / FileLoaderServiceHeartbeat
Registered entry count, usable registry slots and monitor-thread liveness.
.TP
.BR FileLoaderServiceDescribe ( svc ", " bd )
Emit the service state as JSON: configuration, counters and per-file state.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceStop
Signal and reap the monitor thread without tearing the service down.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceGetThread / FileLoaderServiceRegisterThread / FileLoaderServiceUnregisterThread
Monitor-thread handle, and per-consumer-thread attachment for buffer pinning.
.SS Logging \&(logger) \(em \fBlogger/\fP
Records are emitted at eight ordered severity ranks through an opaque
.B UfLogger
handle, to syslog, stderr, stdout, or a size-bounded file.
.PP
The interface names no backend.  A caller states a category, a severity
floor, a destination and an output pattern; the library binds the
implementation and never reports which one it chose.  Records carry the
caller's own source location, and per-thread context can be rendered into
the pattern so that a request identifier set once appears on every record
that request produces.
.PP
Operations a build cannot honour are refused with
.B UF_LOGGER_STATUS_ERR_UNSUPPORTED
rather than silently ignored.
.B UfLoggerGetCapabilities
reports in advance what the bound implementation supports, and
.B UfLoggerDescribeConfiguration
renders the effective configuration as JSON.
.PP
The backend is folded into
.BR libuflib :
a consumer of
.B uflib::uflib
needs no additional include path, no additional link flag, and no knowledge
that a third-party logging library is involved.
.\" ===================================================
.SS QR code \&(qr_code)
Reading and rendering, each behind its own capability so neither drags in the
other's dependency.  Reading uses the vendored
.B quirc
decoder and is built with
.BR UFLIB_CAPABILITY_QUIRC ;
rendering uses libqrencode and is built with
.BR UFLIB_CAPABILITY_QRENCODE .
.PP
.TP
.B UfUtilsQrCodeDecode(image, width, height, stride, text, cap, len)
Read the QR symbol in a greyscale image.
.TP
.B UfUtilsQrCodeCameraGenerate(device, width, height, timeout_ms, text, cap, len)
Open a V4L2 capture device, read a frame from it, and return the decoded text.
.TP
.B UfUtilsQrCodeEncodeText(text, out_matrix)
Encode text as a QR symbol, returned as a module matrix rather than an image.
